//BUG="read_i32 returns 0 when a carriage return or a tab comes before the number"
//IN="1 2 \r\n3\r\n"
//OUT="1 2 3"

// The gap of 58, in the number readers. `read_i64` (src/core/io.cpl) skips
// whitespace with `is_whitespace`, which knows only ' ' and '\n'. The first
// line here ends in a space and then "\r\n": the space ends the 2, and the
// next call starts on the '\r'. That is not whitespace as far as the loop is
// concerned, it is not '-', and it is not a digit, so `read_i64` returns 0
// without having read anything. This prints "1 2 0".
//
// A lone "\r\n" after a number happens to work, because the '\r' is consumed
// as the character that ends the number — which is why CRLF input looks
// fine until a line has a trailing space or the file has a blank line
// ("1\r\n\r\n2" reads 1 and 0). Tabs fail the same way: "1 \t2" reads 1
// and 0.
//
// The wrong value is silent: 0 is a perfectly good number, and every later
// read is shifted by one.

fn main
    a = read_i32()
    b = read_i32()
    c = read_i32()
    out "{a} {b} {c}"
